# Deploy review

Three of the twelve checks need a decision before this ships. The rest are clean.

The interesting finding isn't in the checks at all: most of what the pipeline treats as
configuration is really operational flags, and flags are the things that actually change.

:::migration Not reversible
`0042_drop_legacy_users.sql` has no down step. If the deploy is rolled back, the table is
gone. Either add a down step or split it across two deploys.
:::

:::flags Outside version control
Ten of the seventeen keys in the deploy bundle are operational flags rather than secrets.
Nobody casually rotates a database URI, but people flip flags under pressure — and because
they live outside git, there's no answer to "who changed this, and when?"
:::

## Checks

| Check | Result | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| unit tests | pass | 1,204 in 38s |
| integration | pass | 2 skipped as flaky |
| bundle size | +18% | vendored a date library |
| migration | fail | no down step |

## What changed in CI

```diff
+ OIDC role, branch-scoped
+ flags moved into version control
- static access keys in the runner
```

## Suggested order

1. Add the down step — blocking, about twenty minutes.
2. Split the flags out of the bundle — cheap, and it removes most of the drift.
3. Revisit the bundle size after ship — not urgent.

> Ship one and two together. Step one alone leaves the deploy worse than it started.
